WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.700
"I think painting is the right place for someone
who is both sensitive and ruthless.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.520
Someone who doesn't care if they make their
parents happy or if they earn money."

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:50.100
Daniel Eltinger covers plastic foils in paint
and forms a collage out of them.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.980
One work can take him weeks - sometimes even
months to complete.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.040
“Financially, I never look past the next
month.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.140
That's sometimes OK and sometimes less so.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.160
It means no holidays away and the threat of
poverty in old age.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.660
The rewards of painting are in painting itself.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.020
Having the freedom to live our lives in such
a way that we devote ourselves to this and

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.240
find our fulfilment in it.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.620
There's ecstasy and joy in that - in doing
the right thing.”

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:43.240
Like many artists, Daniel has problems with
the notion of marketing himself for money.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.440
His paintings cost upwards of 1,500 euros
apiece.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.220
The larger works sell for around 6,000.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.420
That may sound like a lot, but he usually
sells only five or six in a year -

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.060
and often has to depend on state welfare to supplement his income.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.720
“This little piece of the world is where
I struggle.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:23.340
The math, the bureaucracy, papers, case workers
and contracts - it's like a house of cards

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.980
that keeps on collapsing.”

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.680
The German capital has become a magnet for
artists from all over the world.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.160
But some make it big.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:58.100
Anyone who exhibits at one of the EIGEN +
ART galleries has made it in the German art world.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:06.500
Owner Gerd Harry Lybke has become famous in
his own right, representing some of the biggest

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.000
names in German contemporary art, like Neo
Rauch.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:21.460
In this show, the gallery walls are bare,
only a bead necklace lies on the floor.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.630
It's part of a 90,000 euro installation.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:28.040
Daniel Eltinger is still struggling to appreciate
what it's all about.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.340
"You are attributing value to all this by
coming here to see it.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.690
There's a connection between the object and
the space and the value that people place

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.720
on the object and the space."

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.520
That may sound very abstract, but it's actually
quite practical.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.020
The gallery is a business, but it's not just
about money...

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.420
"Every artwork and every artist has a story.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.940
And I've been accompanying them for a long
time, so I walk the path with them, a path

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.400
that I wouldn't have taken on my own.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.630
But it's through the artists and their work
that I can find meaning, I discover something

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.660
for my life that is outside my own thinking."

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.860
Daniel likes to go to exhibitions and draw
inspiration from them.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.580
But the commercial art scene is foreign to
him - although he could certainly do with

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.060
being part of it.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.559
“Nowadays there are so many artists that
they can take their pick.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.380
You have to have incredible luck - like in
a fairytale.”

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.980
„The best thing as a young artist is to
open your own gallery.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.580
Or several join together to do it.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.440
We don't take on artists as such.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.000
It's more like 'don't call us, we'll call
you'.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.080
If something catches the team's attention,
or if one of our artists says,

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.600
hey - check this out, then we check it out.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.160
But we don't have time to look through all
the applications that arrive here."

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.160
The art market in Berlin is highly competitive.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:21.920
Supply far outstrips demand.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:29.340
There are more than 20,000 visual artists
in the German capital...

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:48.680
So far Daniel has sold his pictures more or
less by chance - to friends and friends-of-friends

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.120
- and a few collectors.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.740
"I don't think I could paint something in
a particular way just to make it easier to sell.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.020
The viewer should be able to expect that the
painting is genuinely itself.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.380
That it was developed naturally."

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.360
"The freedom to do what I want is the most
precious thing I have....

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:25.760
And so I'm willing to accept that I can't necessarily compete with the others like everyone else does."

